  Still open in M3: the silent-tone gap noted in the audio section above.
  dasm_test and stic_test are M4 scope (the debugger engine), not M3's.

M3 IS COMPLETE (see above for the full FujiNet runtime item that closed it
out).

IN PROGRESS -- M4, debugger engine (CPU side done; STIC/MOB view and
symbols still open):

  [x] core/include/intvdebug.h + core/debugger/debugger.c -- built on
      jzIntv's own hook (cp1600_instr_tick), not a stepped path of our own:
      cp1600_t already calls a registered callback between instructions
      whenever steps_remaining reaches 0, and jzIntv's own console debugger
      (src/debug/debug.c) already registers itself there unconditionally at
      cfg_init() time -- intvdebug_set_engaged(d,1) simply re-registers our
      own hook in its place (a single function pointer, not a chain; safe,
      since the console debugger is never reachable here with no stdin to
      read commands from). Breakpoints use jzIntv's own native mechanism
      (cp1600_set_breakpt/cp1600_clr_breakpt, which substitutes the decoded
      opcode at that address rather than checking a table every
      instruction) including its existing one-shot flag (CP1600_BKPT_ONCE,
      self-clearing on hit) for step_over/step_out's temporary breakpoints.
      Threading matches cocodebug.h's own model exactly: the emulator
      thread blocks (pthread_cond_wait) right inside the hook while paused
      -- there is no other place progress could be stopped, and no
      separate "paused" flag the run loop checks elsewhere.
  [x] FOUND AND FIXED A REAL HANG while building this: cp1600_run() reaches
      its do_instr_tick label -- and so calls the registered hook -- once
      per outer timeslice regardless of step_count (whenever the inner
      instruction loop simply runs out of cycles to spend, not only when
      steps_remaining hits 0), so a naive hook that always blocks
      unconditionally freezes the machine the instant the debugger is
      engaged, before any pause is ever requested. Fixed by blocking only
      when there is an actual reason to (a real/one-shot breakpoint just
      fired, or single-step mode is active) -- verified by debug_test.c
      hanging before the fix and passing cleanly after.
  [x] Also fixed a macro-precedence bug while writing this: cp1600.h's
      CP1600_PK/CP1600_WR substitute their first argument textually into
      `c->periph.bus`, so passing the expression &intv.cp1600 directly (not
      a plain identifier) parsed as &(intv.cp1600->periph.bus) --  wrong
      precedence, a real compile error. Fixed by assigning a local
      `cp1600_t *const cp = &intv.cp1600;` first, matching how every other
      call site of these macros in the staged tree already does it.
  [x] step_over: detects a JSR-family instruction (JSR/JSRE/JSRD/JSRi all
      share that mnemonic prefix, nothing else in the CP-1610 set does) via
      intvdebug_disassemble, then sets a one-shot breakpoint just past it
      and runs free rather than single-stepping through the whole
      subroutine.
  [x] step_out: honestly approximate, and documented as such in
      intvdebug.h -- the CP-1610 has no hardware call stack, so which
      register holds the return address is not recoverable once already
      inside a routine. Breaks on whatever R5 currently holds (the
      convention EXEC and most game software actually use, confirmed
      empirically in debug_test.c: r5 held a plausible EXEC return address
      the whole time). A routine entered through a different register
      falls back to running free rather than stopping somewhere wrong.
  [x] core/tests/debug_test.c -- VERIFIED against the real running
      machine: engage/disengage, pause (waits for the actual instruction
      boundary), register read (PC correctly mirrors r[7], and lands in
      one of the machine's own mapped ROM ranges -- by the time a pause
      actually lands a few ms into boot, execution is well past EXEC init
      and running the embedded config ROM's own code, not stuck in EXEC,
      which is what an earlier, wrong test assumption had to be corrected
      for), disassembly (produces real text at the right address with a
      plausible instruction length), memory read (EXEC ROM is real
      content, not zero-filled) and write (a scratchpad-RAM poke round-
      trips), breakpoint toggle/list, single-step (advances the stop
      serial and re-pauses), resume (leaves the paused state), and a clean
      disengage. All nine tests continue to pass via ctest, clean-room
      rebuild confirmed.

  [x] core/include/intvstic.h + core/debugger/sticview.c -- the STIC
      register/mode/MOB/GRAM-GROM-card/palette view. Every offset and bit
      layout was read directly out of the staged tree's src/stic/stic.c by
      finding where each field is actually consumed (stic_do_mob,
      stic_draw_mobs, stic_ctrl_wr_internal, the raw[0x28-0x32] reads), not
      from a datasheet -- see the header's own comment.
  [x] intvstic_render_backtab -- closes the BACKTAB (background card grid)
      rendering gap noted above. Both STIC modes implemented straight from
      stic_draw_cstk/stic_draw_fgbg's own formulas (line-numbers checked
      against the staged tree, not recalled): Color Stack mode's ordinary
      cards (gr_idx = (card&0xFF8) & (GRAM-select ? gram_mask : ~0), fg_clr
      = ((card>>9)&0x8)|(card&7), cs_idx/bg advance on card bit 13) and its
      "colored squares" special case (card&0x1800==0x1000, four 2x2-pixel
      quadrants, color 7 in any quadrant meaning "current color-stack top" --
      and bit 11 being forced to 0 by the detection mask itself means that
      case can never actually trigger for the fourth quadrant, which the
      code mirrors rather than "fixes"); Foreground/Background mode's
      gr_idx = card&0x9F8, fg_clr = card&7, bg_clr =
      ((card>>9)&0xB)|((card>>11)&0x4).
  [x] core/tests/stic_test.c -- VERIFIED against the real running machine:
      writes known values directly into intv.stic.raw/gmem, then confirms
      the snapshot/decode reports exactly those values back out -- mode,
      GRAM size, full color stack, border color, every MOB field (position,
      both size dimensions, both flips, color, GRAM/priority/visible flags,
      collision bits), an untouched MOB correctly decoding to invisible,
      and a hand-written diagonal bit pattern round-tripping through the
      card-sheet renderer pixel-for-pixel.
  [x] BACKTAB rendering coverage added to stic_test.c -- since
      intvstic_render_backtab is a pure function of the snapshot, these run
      against locally-built snapshots (only intv.gfx.palette, already
      populated by the boot above, is read outside the snapshot):
      Foreground/Background mode's fg/bg pixel colors from a single card;
      Color Stack mode's two ordinary cards proving the stack-advance bit
      actually moves bg from stack slot 0 to slot 1; and the "colored
      squares" special case's four quadrants, including Pix0=7 correctly
      resolving to the live color-stack top rather than a literal color 7.
      All eleven ctest cases pass, clean-room rebuild confirmed in both
      WITH_INTV_ROMS ON and OFF configurations.
  [x] CAUGHT A REAL BUG while writing the card-sheet test: assumed GROM was
      512 cards (matching some other 8-bit VDP-ish chips' convention);
      stic_test.c's first run failed on the card-rendering assertion, which
      led to checking stic_init's own GROM-copy loop
      (`for(i=0;i<2048;i++) stic->gmem[i]=grom_img[i];`) -- GROM is a fixed
      2048 BYTES, i.e. 256 cards at 8 bytes/card, not 512. Fixed in
      intvstic_card_count, the GRAM-offset comment in sticview.c, and the
      test's own offsets; all corrected instances cross-checked against
      that same init loop rather than guessed twice.

  [x] core/debugger/symbols.{c,h} -- name<->address table, modelled
      directly on the CoCo port's own cocosymtab. The built-in table is
      deliberately minimal (just $1000 EXEC_ENTRY and $9C00
      FUJINET_MAILBOX) rather than the usual small library of documented
      ROM entry points CoCo/ADAM/MSX ship: this project has no verified
      primary source in hand for documented Intellivision EXEC subroutine
      addresses (no ROM disassembly, no vendor manual, nothing locally
      checkable), and CoCo's own symtab.c states the principle this
      followed -- "a wrong address in a debugger is worse than a missing
      one" -- so nothing was transcribed from memory with no way to verify
      it. Both built-in entries ARE independently confirmed, from the
      staged jzIntv tree's own source (cp1600_reset's r[7]=0x1000, and
      FUJINET_WINDOW_SIZE's placement in fujinet.h) cross-checked against
      intv_host's own boot log. intvsymtab_load speaks as1600's real -s
      output format, confirmed against jzIntv's own loader for the same
      files (src/debug/debug.c) rather than assumed -- undefined symbols
      ("????????") and out-of-range addresses are skipped exactly as that
      loader does.
  [x] core/tests/symtab_test.c -- VERIFIED (pure function, no emulator
      boot needed): both built-ins, add/lookup in both directions, a real
      as1600-format file (including its undefined-symbol and out-of-range
      skip rules) loading correctly, newest-wins tie-breaking on a shared
      address, and clear_user leaving the built-ins alone.

  M4 IS NOW FULLY COMPLETE, including the BACKTAB rendering gap closed
  above. cp1600dasm.c as a standalone wrapper is still not written (not
  required yet -- intvdebug_disassemble already covers every use so far).

M5 -- GNOME frontend (GTK4 + libadwaita):

  [x] frontends/gnome/{main,window,display}.c -- main window: header bar
      with a menu (Keypad, Debugger, FujiNet Configuration/Console Log,
      About), keyboard capture forwarding everything except F9 (keypad)/
      F11 (fullscreen)/F12 (debugger) through intvsession_key_from_keysym,
      and IntvDisplay -- a GdkMemoryTexture-per-frame widget at
      INTVSESSION_FB_WIDTH x _HEIGHT (fixed geometry, unlike CoCo/MSX's
      runtime-varying viewports -- no per-frame texture resize needed).
      No intvsession_notify_vsync call: unlike CoCo/MSX, intvsession has
      none -- jzIntv's own paced thread already governs itself to NTSC/PAL
      speed independent of the frontend's frame clock.
  [x] frontends/gnome/debugger/dbg_window.c -- poll-based stop tracking
      (intvdebug_stop_serial on a frame-clock tick, modelled on the CoCo
      port's own dbg_window.c -- the engine stops the machine on the
      emulator thread, so a GTK callback from there would be a threading
      bug), with a CPU tab (disassembly with a breakpoint gutter, editable-
      by-poke registers, a memory hex view) and an STIC tab (BACKTAB, all
      8 MOBs, the GRAM/GROM card sheet with pager, palette, and decoded
      register/mode text), laid out like the MSX port's own VDP tab. Owns
      its own intvsymtab instance for disassembly annotation (intvdebug
      has no symbol table of its own, unlike cocodebug).
  [x] frontends/gnome/keypad/keypad_window.c -- the clickable keypad
      sub-window: both hand controllers side by side, each a 3x4 keypad
      grid (raw GtkGestureClick press/release, not GtkButton's "clicked" --
      a keypad digit is a real held button, matching intvsession_pad_key's
      press/release contract), three action buttons, and a 16-position
      direction disc (GtkDrawingArea + GtkGestureClick +
      GtkEventControllerMotion for drag-around, hit-tested geometrically
      by angle rather than 16 separate widgets). Forwards keyboard events
      to the session exactly like the main window does rather than trying
      to refuse focus outright -- GTK4 has no reliable cross-platform way
      to stop a toplevel being focused on click, so typing still drives
      the machine correctly whichever window has focus.
  [x] VERIFIED against the real running app (not just a build check): ran
      the actual GNOME binary under Wayland (grim screenshots), confirmed
      it boots straight to the FujiNet config ROM's HOST SLOTS screen with
      no ROM argument or menu interaction (the core M5 acceptance
      criterion); opened the debugger (INTV_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1 developer
      affordance, matching the sibling repos) and confirmed disassembly/
      registers/memory populate with real paused-machine data and the
      STIC tab's rendered BACKTAB pixel-matches the main display's own
      "HOST SLOTS" text exactly; opened the keypad window
      (INTV_OPEN_KEYPAD=1) and confirmed both controllers render correctly
      including the direction discs. CAUGHT (and fixed) two real bugs this
      way: the frontend executable failed to link ("undefined reference to
      std::locale::~locale") because it only reaches jzintv_core's one
      C++ file (src/locutus/) transitively through intv_session's PUBLIC
      link, one hop too far for CMake's linker-language inference to pick
      up libstdc++ automatically -- fixed by linking stdc++ explicitly in
      frontends/gnome/CMakeLists.txt; and the keypad's number grid/action
      row were left-justified under the "Left/Right Controller" heading
      instead of centered -- fixed with an explicit GTK_ALIGN_CENTER on
      each.
  [x] All 15 ctest cases pass (11 core + 4 new: no_embedded_roms for the
      gnome binary, desktop-entry-validate, appstream metainfo validate,
      plus the existing 11 unaffected). Clean-room rebuild confirmed with
      WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF and the default FRONTEND=all (KDE correctly still
      reports "not implemented yet -- skipping").

  [x] Post-M5 bug fix -- reported symptom: pressing DOWN on the keypad
      window's disc (or a real gamepad) didn't move one item down in
      FujiNet's HOST SLOTS menu, while LEFT rapidly scrolled to the
      bottom. Two real, independent bugs, found by re-deriving the
      disc_codes numbering from the staged jzIntv tree's own mapping.c
      (0=E, 4=N, 8=W, 12=S) and checking each math site against it:
        - frontends/gnome/keypad/keypad_window.c's direction_from_point
          used atan2(dy,dx) directly on SCREEN coordinates (y increasing
          DOWNWARD) without negating dy first, so a click straight below
          the disc's centre computed the same angle a MATH-convention
          upward click would -- it returned index 4 (NORTH) instead of
          12 (SOUTH). Every other direction was unaffected by coincidence
          (E/W sit on the axis dy negation doesn't touch). Fixed with
          atan2(-dy, dx).
        - Both the window's disc and intv_disc_from_stick
          (core/src/gamepad_sdl.c, driving real gamepads) rounded to all
          16 disc_codes positions, including the 8 "half-step" codes
          (e.g. SSW, which OR-combines the S and SW bits) that are
          unreachable from the keyboard by design (intv_keymap.c's DIR_*
          enum only uses the 8 even indices). An analog stick pushed
          toward a pure cardinal wobbles a few degrees off-axis from
          imprecise centering, and a mouse drag toward a cardinal sweeps
          through several fine angles on the way there -- with a 16-way
          16-way split (+-11.25 degrees per cardinal) either could land on
          a half-step, spuriously OR-ing in an adjacent direction's bit
          (e.g. a south-flavoured half-step while pushing pure "left"),
          which is what most likely produced the "rapidly scrolls to the
          bottom" report. Both now round to only the 8 even indices
          (+-22.5 degrees per cardinal), matching what a keyboard can ever
          send.
      core/tests/gamepad_test.c gained assertions for both: a stick 15
      degrees off-axis toward north or south while pushed west still
      resolves to pure west, and every angle in a 3-degree sweep of the
      full circle resolves to an even (non-half-step) code. All 13 ctest
      cases pass; clean-room rebuild confirmed with WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF.
      NOTE: the real-gamepad half of this is a plausible, well-reasoned
      fix but not physically verified on hardware (none attached in the
      environment this was fixed in) -- worth confirming with a real
      controller before considering this fully closed.

  [x] Post-M5 follow-up bug fixes, from real user feedback after the above:
        - The D-pad did nothing on a real gamepad -- core/src/
          gamepad_sdl.c only ever read the left analog stick
          (SDL_GetGamepadAxis), never SDL_GetGamepadButton for
          SDL_GAMEPAD_BUTTON_DPAD_*. Added intv_disc_from_dpad (a pure
          function resolving up to 8 directions from the 4 button
          states, cancelling a directly-opposed pair on one axis to
          neutral), wired into poll_sticks() so the D-pad wins over the
          stick whenever it's actually pressed.
        - "Any small downward nudge on the stick rockets the cursor to
          the bottom of the list" -- traced to the disc math using a
          single deadzone radius for both entering AND leaving a
          direction; boundary wobble (no analog stick sits at a
          perfectly steady raw value) could cross that one radius
          repeatedly, and each crossing is a fresh "just pressed" edge
          to the CONFIG ROM's menu, bypassing its normal typematic
          repeat-delay entirely. Fixed with a two-threshold (enter/exit)
          hysteresis: DEADZONE_ENTER (0.35) to newly register as pushed,
          the narrower DEADZONE_EXIT (0.15) to newly register as
          released again, so boundary wobble while held can't rapid-fire
          press/release transitions.
        - Aspect ratio was too tall/narrow next to real jzIntv --
          frontends/gnome/display.c was letterboxing to the frame
          buffer's own RAW pixel ratio (160/200 = 0.8, portrait), not
          accounting for the STIC's non-square pixels. Real Intellivision
          video (and jzIntv's own default display) fills a standard NTSC
          4:3 picture; hardcoded that as the letterbox aspect instead
          (matching CoCo's own COCO_ASPECT_TV_4_3 reasoning), and widened
          the main window's default size to suit (800x650, was
          640x820).
        - Colors were visibly dimmer than real jzIntv -- root-caused to
          core/jzintv/desktop/gfx_desktop.c (a close copy of the staged
          tree's gfx_null.c, see that file's own header) never
          implementing gfx_sdl2.c's two-phase commit for the STIC's
          video-enable bit: gfx_vid_enable() intentionally sets a
          PENDING flag on a real state change without touching the bit
          actually read (intv_frame_publish's vid_enabled arg, which
          halves RGB when it reads disabled) -- the flip is supposed to
          land once, at the next real redraw, which is exactly what
          gfx_sdl2.c's own refresh path does and gfx_null.c never had to
          (it draws nothing, so nobody was ever hurt by leaving the
          bit un-committed). We DO read that bit for real, so once the
          STIC turned the display on for good, gfx_refresh() kept
          re-arming the pending flag every frame without ever committing
          it -- a permanent, session-long half-brightness read. Added
          the same commit step gfx_sdl2.c has, right before reading the
          bit.
        - The user also asked whether frame pacing might explain the
          repeat-delay oddities -- checked: intv_host.c runs jzIntv's own
          unmodified speed.c throttle (the same real-time regulation
          upstream's own binary uses), so this was not touched; the
          deadzone-hysteresis fix above is the far more likely explanation
          for what was actually observed.
      core/tests/gamepad_test.c gained coverage for intv_disc_from_dpad
      (all 8 directions plus both cancelling-pair cases). All 15 ctest
      cases pass; clean-room rebuild confirmed with WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF.

  [x] CORRECTION to the "frame pacing" line just above -- IT WAS WRONG.
      The user pointed at the actual debug log (jzIntv's own "Rate: [NN%]"
      line) showing >33000%, not ~100%: the CPU really was running
      hundreds of times real NTSC speed, unthrottled. My earlier claim
      that "intv_host.c runs jzIntv's own unmodified speed.c throttle"
      was true but incomplete -- the throttle was never being ENABLED in
      the first place. Root cause: src/cfg/cfg.c defaults
      `cfg->rate_ctl = !plat_is_batch_mode()`, and our plat backend
      (src/plat/plat_null.c, kept as the upstream null driver per
      core/CMakeLists.txt) has `plat_is_batch_mode(void) { return true; }`
      unconditionally -- it has no window to be interactive about, so it
      always reports batch mode, which defaults rate control OFF. A real
      windowed jzIntv build gets rate control for free from plat_sdl.c's
      own (non-batch) plat_is_batch_mode(); we never got the equivalent.
      Fixed in core/jzintv/intv_host.c by adding "-r1" to the argv jzIntv
      is started with, forcing real-time throttle on regardless of the
      batch-mode default -- confirmed via the "Rate:" log line itself
      (stdout is fully buffered when redirected to a file, not
      line-buffered like a TTY, so this needed `stdbuf -oL` and a clean
      SIGTERM shutdown to actually see it rather than losing it to a
      SIGKILL's discarded buffer): before the fix, no "Rate:" line ever
      appeared at all in over 30s of capture (i.e. buffer never even
      filled at that print rate -- consistent with jzp_printf being
      reached far too rarely relative to real elapsed time, though the
      exact mechanism of the 0-lines symptom wasn't chased further once
      -r1 fixed it); after, it settles at 100-117% within a few seconds
      of boot. This is very likely the real explanation for the
      "typematic delay feels off" and probably contributed to the
      deadzone-jitter symptom's severity too (an unthrottled CPU samples
      and acts on stick/disc state far more often per real second than
      intended). All 15 ctest cases pass (no test depended on real-time
      pacing); clean-room rebuild confirmed with WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF.

  [x] Audio was completely missing -- real gap, not a subtle bug:
      intvsession_render_audio() (core/src/session.c) has existed since M3
      as a pull API, but nothing anywhere in the project ever called it.
      No SDL playback device was ever opened; the GNOME frontend never
      referenced audio at all. Fixed by adding core/src/audio_sdl.c
      (modelled directly on the CoCo port's own audio_sdl.c, mono instead
      of stereo -- see intv_audio.h's own comment on why there is no
      stereo signal to fake), opening an SDL3 AudioStream whose pull
      callback drains intvsession_render_audio. Wired into
      intvsession_start/_stop alongside the gamepad subsystem
      (core/src/session.c), registered in core/CMakeLists.txt. Verified
      for real, not just "it compiles": launched the GNOME app, confirmed
      a live PipeWire sink-input under application.name=
      "fujinet-go-intv-gnome", and captured it directly with `pw-cat -r`
      -- 86512 of 96000 captured samples were non-zero, up to amplitude
      10922 (of a 32767 range), i.e. real audible sound actually reaching
      the system's audio device, not silence.
  [x] Follow-up from the same conversation: audio was noticeably behind
      video. Root-caused to core/jzintv/intv_audio.h's ring buffer
      (intv_audio.c): sized generously (0.5s) for a UI thread polling
      once a video frame, but the new SDL audio callback drains it in a
      completely different pattern, and the ring had no mechanism to
      correct a consumer that drains even slightly slower than the
      emulator produces -- every such gap accumulates as backlog, up to
      the full 0.5s ceiling, before the existing plain-overflow drop ever
      triggers. Video has no equivalent problem (intv_frame is a single
      "latest wins" slot, zero possible backlog), so the two drifted
      apart. Note on what "locked together" can actually mean here: true
      sample-accurate genlock between two independently-clocked OS
      subsystems (the display's vsync clock, the audio device's clock)
      isn't something any consumer OS guarantees; what IS achievable, and
      now true here, is that neither side buffers stale data -- both
      track real time as closely as possible and self-correct drift
      rather than accumulate it. Fixed intv_audio_copy to self-correct on
      every call: when more than double the requested amount is
      backlogged, it discards everything except the freshest
      max_samples-worth before copying (trimming only to the 2x trigger
      point, rather than all the way to max_samples, was tried first and
      is WRONG -- it still hands back whatever's oldest within that
      cushion, i.e. still stale; caught by the new test below before ever
      reaching a real build). core/tests/audio_ring_test.c added (pure
      function, no ROMs/emulator boot needed, runs under ctest regardless
      of WITH_INTV_ROMS): publishes a large "stale" batch then a small
      "fresh" batch with no draining in between -- standing in for a
      consumer that fell behind for a while -- and proves a subsequent
      copy returns purely fresh data with nothing stale left over
      afterward, plus confirms ordinary (non-backlogged) operation is
      untouched. All 16 ctest cases pass; clean-room rebuild confirmed
      with WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF.

